I once implemented a version of nethack that did los and lighting in more
reasonable ways.  It was particularly interesting that monsters could 
sneak up behind you in your blind spot.  But overall it did not add much
to the game, and (at that time) the code was slow.  The los code I posted
recently to this group is much better than what I implemented, and would
make this reasonable.

That version of nethack also had odd shaped rooms, overlapping rooms,
rooms with lakes and mazes of columns in them, monsters that would act
as a group, spiders that spun webs, and so on.  It made for an
interesting game.

Since everyone always talks about what should be added:

		Top Five Things To Drop From Nethack

	5. Treasure vaults.  (Boring and mostly ignored.)
	4. Sinks. (A one-shot joke that adds little.)
	3. All anachronisms. (Camera, credit card, ice chest, Hawaian shirt...)
	2. Boulders,statues and gems. (Boulders are an annoyance, statues
	       	are stupid [are there sculptors running around the dungeon?],
		and gems are only useful to anal types who are trying to
		max out the score file.)
	1. Rust monsters. (Just 'cause I hate these.)

And here's the corresponding list:

		Top Five Things to Add to Nethack

	5. Monster-Monster Interactions. (Elves and orcs should duke it
		out, as should dragons/unicorns of different colors,
		and so on.  This business of everyone against the 
		adventurer has got to go.)
	4. Intelligent Monster Behavior. (Dwarves ought to grab treasure
		and take it back to hide somewhere in a secret room
		they've carved out, hobbits should set traps in front of
		doors and then shoot you full of arrows while you're stuck,
		monsters that think you look tough should run away,
		monsters should use the items they find lying around the
		dungeon, and so on.)
	3. Bargaining. (Not shopkeepers, but monsters and you.  Maybe you
		could offer that demon who's about to kill you a named
		weapon to spare your life, or a hobbit you're about to kill
		could offer you the gold he's carrying, or to lead you
		to a treasure, etc.)
	2. Better Magic System. (There's been some good discussion on this
		topic, sufficient to say that the current version is lame.)
	1. Better Character Generation.  (Since everyone starts over and 
		over until they get what they want, that should just be 
		included in the game.)


